        "body": "Stubbing a function with an ordinary stub while the same function is also the target of `#[kani::proof_for_contract]` or `#[kani::stub_verified]` fails with an internal compiler error instead of a diagnostic.\n\n## Reproduce\n\n```rust\n#[kani::requires(x < 100)]\n#[kani::ensures(|r: &u32| *r == x + 1)]\nfn f(x: u32) -> u32 { x + 1 }\n\nfn g(x: u32) -> u32 { x.wrapping_add(1) }\n\nfn caller(x: u32) -> u32 { f(x) }\n\n/// Stub the function under verification.\n#[kani::proof_for_contract(f)]\n#[kani::stub(f, g)]\nfn check_target_stubbed() { let x = kani::any(); let _ = f(x); }\n\n/// Stub a function that is also replaced by its contract.\n#[kani::proof]\n#[kani::stub_verified(f)]\n#[kani::stub(f, g)]\nfn verified_and_stubbed() { let x: u32 = kani::any_where(|x| *x < 100); let _ = caller(x); }\n```\n\n```\nkani main.rs -Z function-contracts -Z stubbing --harness check_target_stubbed\n```\n\nBoth harnesses fail with:\n\n```\nerror: Failed to find contract closure `__kani_recursion_check_f` in function `main::f`\n```\n\nThe stubbing pass replaces `f`'s body with `g`'s, and the contract pass then looks for `f`'s generated closures in that body. This happens on `main` and with #5204. That PR fixes the ordinary case (`#[kani::stub(f, g)]` alone, #4591) but intentionally leaves these two combinations unchanged.\n\n## Expected\n\nBoth combinations are configuration mistakes: the first stubs the function under verification, the second stubs a function that is replaced by its contract. Kani should reject them with an error that names the conflicting attributes, e.g. \"`f` cannot be both the target of `proof_for_contract`/`stub_verified` and replaced by `#[kani::stub(f, g)]`\".\n",
        "closed_by": null,
